Session 01a1cffb-4de1-75e6-b949-1e92d45a76cf. Plan: plan/2026-11-02-push-pull-dispatch-fanout-parity.md (+ .zh-tw.md, diagrams/02-pull-dispatch-fanout.svg). Commit prefix [CEN-1637]. Toolchain: rust 1.85.0 pinned by rust-toolchain.toml; cargo/tokio 1.49.0 per Cargo.lock.

Plan files were already revised in the working tree (owner-supplied). Committed unchanged as `[CEN-1637] docs: revise pull dispatch fan-out parity plan`; the plan itself is never edited during implementation.

Phase 4.1 + 4.4 dispatcher work (`crates/rocketmq-classic-consumer/src/push/pull_service.rs`):
Decision: replace `PULL_RPC_WORKER_COUNT = 4` / `PULL_RPC_QUEUE_CAPACITY = 64` and the shared mpsc receiver with an owned `tokio::task::JoinSet<()>`. `PullRpcWorkerSet` now stores `Weak<PushConsumer>`, `Arc<PullScheduler>`, the watch sender, the JoinSet and a separate `Arc<AtomicUsize>` active counter; `None` means admission is closed. Chose JoinSet over a `HashMap<PullIdentity, JoinHandle>` because a self-removing handle map can detach an unfinished task's tail and misses abort-before-first-poll cleanup.
Decision: `PullCompletionGuard` (request + scheduler + active counter) is constructed before `JoinSet::spawn` and moved into the future, so completion, failed `Weak` upgrade, panic unwinding, watch cancellation and abort-before-first-poll each release exactly one scheduler slot; the guard never touches dispatcher state and holds no strong consumer reference. The pull future is scoped inside `run_owned_pull` so it drops before the guard, and the receiver's current value is checked before the biased `select!` so a task first polled after cancellation issues no RPC.
Decision: `try_submit` keeps one lock acquisition by delegating to private generic `submit_with(owner_weak, request)`; under that lock it rejects absent state, reaps finished tasks with `JoinSet::try_join_next` (panics reported, cancellations ignored), clones the cancellation/consumer/scheduler/counter, increments the counter, builds the guard and spawns. No await and no scheduler call occurs while the dispatcher lock is held. Verified `PullService`'s owned pull worker invokes `ConsumerRuntime::pull_message` inside a Tokio task (`crates/rocketmq-classic-core/src/runtime/pull.rs`), so spawning from admission is legal; that stage's unbounded FIFO is unchanged.
Decision: `stop(wait_millis)` takes the set (atomically closing admission), signals cancellation, joins with `join_next` under one deadline, then `abort_all` and drains all join results so guards run. `Drop` signals cancellation and lets the owned JoinSet abort; cleanup is observed when the runtime polls the aborts, not synchronously in `Drop`. Test accessor `worker_count()` replaced by `active_admissions()` reading the separate counter (JoinSet::len counts completed unjoined tasks); `PullScheduler::worker_count()` unchanged.
Rust 1.85 constraint: `let`-chains are unstable on this pinned toolchain, so join-result classification uses a small `report_join` match helper instead of `if let ... && ...`.
Tests (same file, `mod tests`): replaced the fixed-set test with admission/concurrency/lifecycle coverage driven through `schedule`/`take_due` identity admission and the same guard/spawn/join path as production via private `submit_with` (no public testing API): 80 distinct queue identities admitted and proven concurrently entered on a release barrier plus retained active-counter witness; deferred follow-up released exactly once with raw scheduler `in_flight` inspected while the scheduler is still running; expired-`Weak` production path; panic and watch-cancellation join observation; abort of a polled pending task; abort before first poll (no yield between admission and zero-budget stop); dispatcher `Drop`; admission racing stop with a zero wait budget where each request is either owned-and-drained or returned to the caller; retiring-generation completion cannot release a new generation's slot; completed entries reaped across 200 rounds (`JoinSet::len` stays bounded). A test-only `Debug for PullRequest` was added so admission results can be unwrapped without changing production types.
Call-site audit (§4.3): `rg -n 'PullRpcWorkers::new|pull_rpc_workers|worker_count\(\)|BROKER_SUSPEND_MAX_TIME_MILLIS' crates/rocketmq-classic-consumer/src` shows only dispatcher-count assertions changed: `src/push/consumer/tests.rs` startup expectation now zero active admissions before any assignment, and the three shutdown assertions renamed to `active_admissions()`. Consume-service and scheduler count assertions kept. Startup/shutdown acquisition order unchanged (`PullSchedulerStarted` -> `PullRpcWorkersStarted`, rollback and shutdown keep scheduler-stop then dispatcher-stop); no signature change; POP path untouched in this phase.
Checks: `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-consumer --lib push::pull_service::tests --locked` PASS 17/17 (plus 289 filtered). `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-consumer --lib --locked` PASS 306/306. `cargo fmt --all` applied; `cargo clippy -p rocketmq-classic-consumer --all-targets --locked` clean.

Phase 4.2 hand-off, RPC mode and Pull suspend constant:
Decision: `PushConsumerRegistration::pull_message` (`src/push/consumer/runtime.rs`) now releases a rejected request with `PullScheduler::finish` only; the previous path also re-queued it at `PULL_TIME_DELAY_MILLIS_WHEN_CACHE_FLOW_CONTROL` (50 ms), which is an extra hand-off retry that has no Java counterpart and hides closed admission. The 50 ms constant and its genuine ProcessQueue cache-flow-control uses are untouched (`src/push/consumer/processing.rs`, POP worker path).
Plan-vs-branch discrepancy: the plan names `PullServiceError::Closed` and a broker `PROCESSING_REQUEST_OVERFLOW` retry inside runtime-level `pull_message`. Neither exists on this branch: `rg -n 'PULL_TIME_DELAY_MILLIS_WHEN_OVERFLOW|PullServiceError' crates` and `rg -n 'PROCESSING_REQUEST_OVERFLOW' crates` return no hits. Push pull failures here map through `PullAttemptError::from_rpc_with_delay` (20 ms for `response::FLOW_CONTROL`, otherwise the exception delay) into one delayed scheduler follow-up, i.e. exactly one RPC per admission already. Nothing was removed beyond the closed-admission re-queue; there is no overflow retry test to keep, so a new runtime-level regression was added instead of deleting an existing one.
Decision: `fetch_push_result` (`src/push/consumer/processing.rs`) now calls `MQClientAPI::pull_message_future` (Java ASYNC pull, `RemotingClient::invoke`, response wait at deadline + transport cleanup grace) instead of `pull_message` (`invoke_sync_with_deadline`, socket close on timeout). The absolute 30 s deadline from `CONSUMER_TIMEOUT_MILLIS_WHEN_SUSPEND` is preserved and no core API or other caller changed.
Decision: added `PULL_BROKER_SUSPEND_MAX_TIME_MILLIS = 15_000` in `src/push/consumer.rs` for the Pull header's `suspendTimeoutMillis`; `BROKER_SUSPEND_MAX_TIME_MILLIS = 20_000` remains only as the POP worker's long-poll wait feeding its `pollTime + 10s` deadline.
Plan-vs-branch discrepancy: §4.4 asks to "retain a POP assertion that pollTime == 20000". No such assertion exists on this branch and `run_pop_worker` never sets `PopMessageRequestHeader.poll_time`, so the wire field encodes the default `0`; asserting 20000 would require an unsanctioned POP wire change (§4.2 keeps POP unchanged). Instead `pull_and_pop_keep_separate_broker_suspend_values` pins Pull 15000 / POP 20000 / client timeout 30000 so the two paths cannot be merged again, and the Pull wire value is asserted from scripted peers.
Tests added in `src/push/consumer/tests.rs`: `closed_pull_admission_releases_the_slot_without_an_rpc_attempt` (runtime-level: slot released via new cfg(test) `PullScheduler::in_flight_count`, no re-queue, zero active admissions); `one_pull_attempt_is_made_per_admission_and_a_follow_up_waits_for_release` (started consumer + scripted nameserver/broker peers, SYSTEM_ERROR reply, exactly one PULL_MESSAGE per admission, `suspendTimeoutMillis == 15000` on the wire, follow-up invisible while in flight and exactly one scheduled after release); `push_pull_uses_the_future_rpc_mode_within_the_thirty_second_deadline` (`start_paused`, scripted peer answers at deadline + 500 ms inside the async transport grace: future mode succeeds where sync mode would be cut off; Pull header suspend asserted on the wire); `pull_and_pop_keep_separate_broker_suspend_values`. The existing `push_pull_request_uses_live_batch_settings_from_consumer_config` scripted peer now also asserts `suspendTimeoutMillis == 15000`.
Checks: `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-consumer --lib push:: --locked` PASS 161/161; `cargo clippy -p rocketmq-classic-consumer --all-targets --locked` clean; `cargo fmt --all` applied.

Phase 4.5 scripted-peer integration test: `crates/rocketmq-classic-consumer/tests/push_pull_fanout.rs` (new).
Design: one nameserver peer answers GET_ROUTEINFO_BY_TOPIC with a single broker-a route carrying 16 read/write queues for the target topic and an empty route for every other topic, so retry-topic traffic cannot enter the barrier. One broker peer splits each accepted connection into independent reader/writer tasks; PULL_MESSAGE frames are recorded as (connection, opaque) -> queueId and left unanswered until a broadcast release round arrives, so held responses never block later reads on the same connection. HEART_BEAT bodies supply the live clientID, which GET_CONSUMER_LIST_BY_GROUP echoes back so real clustering rebalance assigns all 16 queues to the single consumer. QUERY_CONSUMER_OFFSET answers QUERY_NOT_FOUND (no committed offset) and the ConsumeFromWhere lookup replies carry an `offset` field; a SUCCESS reply without that field is rejected by `required_response_header_field`, which was the first failure observed while bringing the peer up.
Assertions: round 1 requires all queue ids 0..15 outstanding exactly once within 5 s with distinct (connection, opaque) identities and `suspendTimeoutMillis == 15000` on every header, plus a rejection of any further admission before a response is sent; one release round then requires the same 16-queue fan-out again from fresh request identities, proving slot release and scheduler rescheduling; shutdown with `shutdown_with_await_termination_millis(50)` while the second round is unanswered must unregister, admit no later Pull, and finish peer tasks within bounded waits.
Regression evidence: with the pre-change dispatcher restored (`git show bbd6b58e:.../pull_service.rs`) the same test fails with `only 4 of 16 queues started a Pull within 5s`; with the owned per-request dispatcher it passes in 1.79 s.
Checks: `cargo fmt --all`; `cargo clippy -p rocketmq-classic-consumer --all-targets --locked` clean; `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-consumer --test push_pull_fanout --locked` PASS 1/1; `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-consumer --locked` PASS (310 lib, 92 integration incl. doc-tests 12).

Phase 4.6 documentation (part 1): added `docs/architecture/ADR-0085-push-pull-dispatch-fanout.md` (0085 was unused; the highest existing record is ADR-0084). It records the superseded Phase 10 four-task pool decision, the JoinSet + pre-spawn guard ownership contract and why a self-removing handle map was rejected, closed-admission slot release without an overflow retry, future/async Pull selection under the absolute 30 s deadline, the 15 s Pull vs 20 s POP split, and the qualified transport claim (Java's async semaphore default 65,535, so parity is "one outstanding poll per eligible queue while permits remain", not unlimited concurrency).
Updated `docs/compatibility/PHASE_10_OFFLINE_METHOD_MATRIX.md` Pull scheduling row, Pull header row and shutdown row: the scheduling row no longer claims bounded RPC workers and now cites ADR-0085 plus the scripted-peer barrier result (old pool fails at 4 of 16); the pullMessage row records the future/async API choice and the 15 s/30 s vs POP 20 s split; the shutdown row states the close-admission, cancel, join-to-budget, abort-and-drain order with exactly-once guard release.
Updated the shared diagram `plan/2026-11/diagrams/02-pull-dispatch-fanout.svg`: middle lane relabelled as the removed pre-change pool ("sync RPC; Pull header reused POP's 20 s"), latency note marked illustration-only, target lane relabelled as implemented with future RPC and 15 s / 30 s bounds, plus closed-admission and stop-order notes.
`docs/compatibility/JAVA_5_5_1_COMPATIBILITY.md` Phase 10 evidence rows and a new `CURRENT_STATUS.md` section are updated together with the P10-V05 registration so the pending live case is described once.

Phase 4.7 permanent P10-V05 differential runner (no live Broker run executed in this session):
Checker `tests/differential/check_p10_fanout.py` (new). Evidence contract: each probe writes `<language>-p10-fanout.tsv` with tab rows `P10F ROUTE|PULL_START|PULL_END|GATE|SEND_START|SEND_END|DELIVERY|SHUTDOWN`; every timestamp is that probe process's own monotonic nanoseconds and no clock is compared across processes. Checks per language independently: route inventory must be exactly queues 0..queues-1 on `<topic-base>_<language>`; every target Pull must request `suspendTimeoutMillis=15000`; one GATE row whose distinct queue count equals the manifest queue count and whose span is inside the admission window; first-start span inside the window and no target Pull response completing before the last first-start (the fan-out barrier); a start/end event sweep proving at most one active Pull per queue and that each later attempt starts only after the previous completion; unique opaques with no orphan responses; no Pull admission after the SHUTDOWN row; exactly one seeded send and exactly one delivery per queue, delivered on the same queue inside the delivery window.
Decision: shutdown may legitimately leave a long-poll unanswered because no response hook fires, so exact start/end multiset equality was replaced by an orphan-response check plus a tolerance of exactly one unmatched admission per queue, allowed only when it is that queue's latest Pull; more than one outstanding Pull on a queue, or a later completion while an earlier one stayed outstanding, still fails. `check_language` returns `(failures, elapsed_ms, in_flight)` and the summary prints `outstanding_at_shutdown=N[queue_ids]`.
Checker checks: `python3 tests/differential/check_p10_fanout.py --self-test` PASS (11 synthetic cases, incl. outstanding-at-shutdown pass and two-outstanding-one-queue fail). Contract test with generated evidence in /tmp using the exact runner form `check_p10_fanout.py <dir> compat_p10_fanout --queues=16`: fully-completed variant PASS exit 0; one-in-flight variant PASS exit 0 with `outstanding_at_shutdown=1[3]`.
Java probe `compat/java-reference/src/main/java/compat/LivePullFanoutProbe.java` (new) + Maven execution id `live-pull-fanout-probe`: uses the 5.5.1 `DefaultMQPushConsumer(group, RPCHook)` constructor and records only target-topic/target-group `RequestCode.PULL_MESSAGE` boundaries from `doBeforeRequest`/`doAfterResponse`, falling back to `RemotingCommand.getExtFields()` when no typed custom header is present on the decoded command; gates on 16 distinct queue admissions inside 5 s, writes the ready file, then seeds one message per queue with `DefaultMQProducer.send(msg, MessageQueue)` and waits for 16 deliveries. Compile fix: `fetchPublishMessageQueues` returns `List<MessageQueue>`, not `Set<MessageQueue>`. An initial pom edit produced duplicate `<execution>` tags; after repair `python3 -c "xml.etree.ElementTree.parse(...)"` reports OK and `mvn -o -q -DskipTests compile -f compat/java-reference/pom.xml` exits 0.
Rust probe subcommand `live_client p10-fanout <namesrv> <topic> <group> <ready-file>` in `integration-tests/src/bin/live_client.rs`: `PushConsumer::new_with_rpc_hook` with a `FanoutRpcHook` that mirrors the Java hook pair, `target_pull_header` trying `read_custom_header::<PullMessageRequestHeader>()` then `ext_fields`, probe-local monotonic nanosecond base, ROUTE inventory from `fetch_publish_message_queues`, same 5 s admission gate and violation detection, per-queue seeding with `send_to_queue`, delivery wait inside the 5 s window, then SHUTDOWN. Producer config mirrors the Java probe (`set_send_message_with_vip_channel(false)`, `set_send_timeout_millis(10_000)`); the repo-wide probe convention `mq_client_api_timeout = 5_000` was kept after confirming it does not cap Push Pull: `rpc_timeout()` is only used for route/heartbeat/control paths (`crates/rocketmq-classic-core/src/runtime/runtime.rs`, `heartbeat.rs`, `runtime/client_check.rs`, `runtime/control.rs`) while the Push pull deadline comes from `CONSUMER_TIMEOUT_MILLIS_WHEN_SUSPEND` in `src/push/consumer/processing.rs`.
Runner registration: new manifest row after P10-V03 with profile `host-routed`, inputs topic/group/queues=16/messages_per_queue=1/admission_window_ms=5000/delivery_window_ms=5000 plus the five checks mirrored into assertions, both client directions, cleanup listing base and derived topics/groups, and `"live_status": "PENDING_PHASE_19"`. Manifest validator initially failed with `scenario cleanup omits input resources for P10-V05`; fixed by listing the base topic/group alongside the `_java`/`_rust` derivations, while `run.sh` overrides the actual topic cleanup list to only the derived topics and adds a separate `fanout_groups_created` trap that deletes `${consumer_group}_java`/`${consumer_group}_rust`. `run.sh` also gained: the implemented-scenario entry, the long negative guard condition, the `host-routed:P10-V05` profile case, the `P10-V05:compat_p10_fanout` metadata gate (profile/group/queues), a python input gate pinning windows and checks, derived-topic creation (`-r 16 -w 16`), derived-group creation, a commands transcript, and an execution block that runs the Java probe then the Rust probe sequentially on separate topics/groups so the two clients never share a group, then invokes `"$repo_root/tests/differential/check_p10_fanout.py" "$evidence_dir" "$topic" --queues="$queue_count"` (the checker path was made `$repo_root`-anchored to match every other execution invocation).
Runner checks without any Docker resource: `bash -n tests/differential/run.sh` OK; `python3 tests/differential/compare.py --validate-manifest` -> Validated 38 scenarios; `compare.py --self-test` OK; `run.sh --list | grep P10-V05` shows host-routed / compat_p10_fanout / compat_p10_push_fanout / 16; `run.sh --check-all` -> "Every selected live scenario has a local runner (38 rows); no Docker resources were started."; profile guards refuse without `ROCKETMQ_CLASSIC_TEST_ENV` and with `ROCKETMQ_PROFILE=basic` ("must explicitly equal the manifest profile 'host-routed'", exit 2). `cargo fmt --all` applied; `cargo clippy -p rocketmq-classic-integration --bin live_client --locked` clean (one fix: `ProducerConfig::new(format!(...))` avoids needless borrow; a mistaken `?` on `Producer::new` was reverted because that constructor is infallible).

